[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Securing GraphQL APIs: Rate Limiting, Depth Limiting, and Cost Analysis"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unlike REST APIs, GraphQL allows clients to request precisely the data they need in a single round trip. However, this flexibility introduces unique security challenges. A malicious actor can craft deeply nested queries or request massive amounts of data, leading to Denial of Service (DoS) attacks. This tutorial covers three essential security layers for GraphQL: Rate Limiting, Depth Limiting, and Cost Analysis."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Rate Limiting in GraphQL"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Traditional REST rate limiting relies on counting HTTP requests (e.g., 100 requests per hour per IP). In GraphQL, a single HTTP request can contain a massive workload or multiple operations. Therefore, GraphQL rate limiting must account for the complexity of the query, not just the count of HTTP requests."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"const rateLimit = require('graphql-rate-limit-directive');

const typeDefs = `
  type Query {
    user(id: ID!): User @rateLimit(max: 10, window: "60s")
  }
`;"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Depth Limiting"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Nested queries are a powerful feature, but unbounded nesting can crash your server due to recursive database lookups or stack overflows. Depth limiting restricts how deep a query can be nested before it is executed."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"const depthLimit = require('graphql-depth-limit');

const server = new ApolloServer({
  typeDefs,
  resolvers,
  validationRules: [depthLimit(3)]
});"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"A depth limit of 3 allows Query -> author -> posts -> comments, but rejects any deeper nesting."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Depth limiting runs during the validation phase before any database queries are generated, making it very efficient."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Cost Analysis (Complexity Analysis)"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sometimes a query is not deeply nested, but requests thousands of items at the root level. Cost analysis assigns a weight or cost to every field, argument, and type in your schema. Before executing the query, the total cost is calculated against a maximum threshold."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"const { createComplexityLimitRule } = require('graphql-validation-complexity');

const server = new ApolloServer({
  typeDefs,
  resolvers,
  validationRules: [
    createComplexityLimitRule(1000, {
      onCost: (cost) => console.log("Query Cost:", cost),
      objectCost: 2,
      scalarCost: 1,
      listFactor: 10
    })
  ]
});"},{"order":14,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Securing a production-ready GraphQL API requires more than traditional IP-based rate limiting. By implementing depth limiting to stop recursive abuse and cost analysis to measure resource consumption, you can protect your infrastructure from malicious queries and accidental over-fetching."}]
